>
  <p>Seven days is not ideal, but it is enough for a strong result if you spend it on the right things. The secret is not studying more hours; it is <strong>choosing what to study</strong> on day 1, and <strong>testing yourself</strong> instead of rereading for the rest of the week.</p>

  <h2 id="possible">Can you really prepare in a week?</h2>
  <p>Yes, for most tests, if you accept one trade-off: you will secure the high-mark and familiar topics well, rather than everything badly. A week also gives you time for a few rounds of spaced review, which research shows beats cramming the same hours together. For a test one week away, the best gap between reviews of a topic was roughly 20–40% of that week, so about <strong>1.5 to 3 days</strong> (Cepeda et al., 2008).</p>

  <h2 id="plan">The 7-day plan</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Day</th><th scope="col">Focus</th><th scope="col">Output by the evening</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Day 1</strong></td><td>Triage + first topics</td><td>Topic list ranked by marks × weakness; top 2 topics studied and self-tested</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Day 2</strong></td><td>Learn topics 3–5</td><td>Notes from memory for each; 10-minute quiz on day-1 topics</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Day 3</strong></td><td>Learn topics 6–8 + review day 1</td><td>Practice questions on day-1 topics (spaced review)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Day 4</strong></td><td>Learn remaining priority topics + review day 2</td><td>All priority topics covered once</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Day 5</strong></td><td>Mixed practice</td><td>Previous-year questions across all topics, mixed together</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Day 6</strong></td><td>Full timed paper</td><td>One paper in exam conditions, marked, every mistake fixed</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Day 7</strong></td><td>Light review</td><td>One-page summary sheet; mistakes list reviewed; early night</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <h3>How to do the day-1 triage</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>Write down every topic from the syllabus or chapter list.</li>
    <li>Give each topic a <strong>marks score</strong> (1–3, from past papers or weightage) and a <strong>weakness score</strong> (1–3).</li>
    <li>Multiply. Study the highest scores first. Topics scoring 1–2 get only a quick read on day 7, or are dropped.</li>
    <li>Cut 20% from your plan now. People reliably underestimate how long work takes (Buehler et al., 1994), and a plan with slack is one you will finish.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="day">What each study day looks like</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Block</th><th scope="col">Time</th><th scope="col">Task</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1</td><td>50 min</td><td>New topic: read once, actively, making short notes</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2</td><td>50 min</td><td>Close notes, write everything you remember, check, then do 5–10 questions</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3</td><td>50 min</td><td>Next topic, same pattern</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4</td><td>40 min</td><td>Spaced review of a topic from 2 days ago, using questions not notes</td></tr>
      <tr><td>5</td><td>30 min</td><td>Flashcards + update the mistakes list; plan tomorrow</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Take 10-minute breaks between blocks, away from your phone. If starting is hard, use 25-minute <a href="/tools/pomodoro-timer/">Pomodoro</a> rounds instead.</p>

  <h2 id="methods">Methods that work fast (and ones that do not)</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Use</th><th scope="col">Avoid</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Writing what you remember with the book closed</td><td>Rereading the chapter again</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Past papers and previous-year questions</td><td>Highlighting whole pages</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Flashcards you test yourself on</td><td>Copying notes neatly</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Mixing topics in a practice set</td><td>Twenty of the same question type in a row</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Explaining a topic aloud to a friend</td><td>Watching long videos at normal speed</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Why the left column wins: in Roediger and Karpicke’s 2006 experiment, students who practised recalling a passage remembered <strong>61%</strong> of it a week later, against <strong>40%</strong> for students who reread it, even though rereading felt more effective at the time. A large review rated practice testing and spaced practice as the two most useful techniques, and highlighting and rereading as low-utility (Dunlosky et al., 2013).</p>

  <h2 id="night">The night before and exam morning</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Stop new learning by early evening.</strong> Review your summary sheet and mistakes list only.</li>
    <li><strong>Sleep your normal hours.</strong> High-school students who traded sleep for extra study had more trouble in class and on tests the next day (Gillen-O’Neel et al., 2013).</li>
    <li><strong>Pack the night before:</strong> admit card or ID, pens, calculator if allowed, water.</li>
    <li><strong>Exam morning:</strong> 15–20 minutes with your summary sheet, then stop. Arrive early.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Next time, start the plan earlier: the <a href="/guides/study-plan-for-exams/">backward planning method</a> gives you three full rounds instead of one. Add your exam to the ClassTrack app and it counts down on your home screen, so the next one never arrives as a surprise.</p>
</Guide>
